Actually, let me look at the `ParseLV2MaterialBlock` more carefully. The key issue might be with the `NUMSUBMTLS` handling:

```cpp
if (TokenMatch(filePtr, "NUMSUBMTLS", 10)) {
    ParseLV4MeshLong(iNumSubMaterials);
    mat.avSubMaterials.resize(iNumSubMaterials, Material("INVALID SUBMATERIAL"));
}
```

If `iNumSubMaterials` is huge, `mat.avSubMaterials.resize()` would try to allocate a huge vector. But the crash is `UNKNOWN READ`, not allocation failure.

Wait, let me look at the `SUBMATERIAL` handling:

```cpp
if (TokenMatch(filePtr, "SUBMATERIAL", 11)) {
    unsigned int iIndex = 0;
    ParseLV4MeshLong(iIndex);
    if (iIndex >= iNumSubMaterials) {
        LogWarning("Out of range: submaterial index is too large");
        iIndex = iNumSubMaterials - 1;
    }
    if (iIndex < mat.avSubMaterials.size()) {
        Material &sMat = mat.avSubMaterials[iIndex];
        ParseLV2MaterialBlock(sMat);
    }
    continue;
}
```

If `iNumSubMaterials` is 0 and `SUBMATERIAL` is encountered, `iIndex >= 0` is true, so `iIndex = 0xFFFFFFFF`. Then `iIndex < mat.avSubMaterials.size()` is false (since `avSubMaterials` is empty), so no crash.

But what if `iNumSubMaterials` is 1 and `SUBMATERIAL 0` is encountered? `iIndex = 0`, `0 >= 1` is false, so `sMat = mat.avSubMaterials[0]`. Then `ParseLV2MaterialBlock(sMat)` is called recursively. This can recurse deeply if the input has nested `SUBMATERIAL` blocks.

The crash is in the copy constructor during vector reallocation. This could happen when `avSubMaterials` is resized and needs to reallocate. During reallocation, existing sub-materials are moved. If a sub-material has corrupted data...

